#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074630} :activity/id #uuid "8d68098e-61ea-5694-8c81-2a07b3b89448" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050924 :topic/id #uuid "7247d928-5458-5a46-a08c-b035b4eadd8a" :topic/math-academy-id 534} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075037 :tutorial/content "A **ratio** is a mathematical statement that expresses the relative sizes of two quantities.\n\nAs an example, let's look at the triangles and circles below.\n\n![](images/48/4865786ce55e0ec3fc1747ece4ea962425a3f83b2a45c7980f52719522318c82.png)\n\nWe can express the ratio of the number of circles to the number of triangles as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{number of circles}}}: {\\color{red}{\\textrm{number of triangles}}}\n$$\n\nTo find this ratio, we start by counting the number of occurrences of each shape:\n\n- First, we count the number of circles. There are $\\color{blue}5$ circles.\n- Next, we count the number of triangles. There are $\\color{red}2$ triangles.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of circles to triangles is ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}2}$.\n\nWe can describe this ratio in words as follows:\n\n> *The ratio of circles to triangles is ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}2}$ because there are ${\\color{blue}5}$ circles*for every*${\\color{red}{2}}$ triangles.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "3d8ebe22-8ef7-538a-a42d-4f1133689833" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8277 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0340ef01-84ac-534c-a79b-d7336d5305b6" :step/math-academy-id 185365 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074631}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073450 :question/id #uuid "c4235bf2-0c2b-5c2d-be8d-779a8e5fb38f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13256"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be25cd8-4333-5e00-91e9-7e1256281814" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048698}] :knowledge-point/questions